    "finish_elapsed_s": 7.111243665975053,
    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. Inside the fridge, the refrigerant evaporates and absorbs heat from your food, making the inside cold. Then a compressor squeezes the refrigerant, turning it into a hot gas. That heat travels through coils on the back or bottom of the fridge and escapes into your kitchen. Finally, the refrigerant cools down, turns back into a liquid, and starts the cycle again. So a fridge doesn't really \"make cold\"—it removes heat, over and over, keeping your milk and leftovers chilly."
